      <p class="font-semibold text-lg mb-2">Direct Answer</p>
      <p>For tall users between 6'0" and 6'2", the Herman Miller Aeron Size C is the stronger pick — its mesh back, PostureFit SL sacral support, and 20.5" max seat height cover that bracket well. At 6'3" and above, the Steelcase Leap Plus is the better fit — ordered with the optional 5" cylinder: its 22.5" max seat height in that configuration, independently adjustable lumbar, and 500 lb weight capacity address the dimensional gaps that make the Aeron Size C a tight fit for taller frames. With the standard cylinder the Leap Plus stops at 19.5", below the Aeron, and the recommendation reverses.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ANSWER-FIRST OPENING -->
    <p>The Aeron Size C and Steelcase Leap Plus are the two most-researched ergonomic chairs among tall buyers — and for good reason: both are engineered for larger users, both carry premium price tags, and both appear on nearly every "best chairs for tall people" list. The decision between them comes down to one number more than any other: seat height. And for the Leap Plus that number is not fixed — it depends on which gas cylinder was ordered. With the optional 5" cylinder the Leap Plus reaches 22.5" at its maximum, giving a 6'4" user with a 19" popliteal height room to set the seat correctly without raising hip angle above 90°. With the standard cylinder it stops at 19.5", below the Aeron, and loses this comparison outright. The Aeron Size C tops out at 20.5", which works at 6'2" but creates mechanical disadvantage at greater heights. Everything else — lumbar design, seat depth, armrest range — follows from that foundational difference.</p>

    <!-- SPEC TABLE -->
    <h2>Side-by-Side Specs: Aeron Size C vs Leap Plus</h2>

