    <!-- tca-aio-capsule -->
    <p class="citation-capsule">The Herman Miller Aeron Size C has a seat height range of 16.0"–20.5" and a back height of 25.5", making it well-suited for users between 6'0" and 6'2". The Steelcase Leap Plus extends to a 22.5" maximum seat height when ordered with its optional 5" cylinder — 15.5"–19.5" with the standard cylinder — and pairs that with a 25.5" back height and a 500 lb weight capacity, giving the correctly specified configuration a clear dimensional edge for users 6'3" and taller. For buyers using popliteal height as the primary fit criterion — as ergonomic guidelines recommend — the Leap Plus covers a wider range of tall-user leg lengths, while the Aeron Size C offers a superior mesh seating surface and a more established lumbar support profile for mid-range tall users.</p>

    <!-- HEIGHT-BRACKET RECOMMENDATIONS -->
    <h2>Height-Bracket Fit Recommendations</h2>

    <p>Fit isn't a single answer — it depends on where in the tall-user range you fall. The following breakdown uses seat height and lumbar positioning as the primary variables, consistent with ANSI/HFES 100 ergonomic guidelines for seated workstation configuration.</p>

    <h3>6'0" – 6'2": Aeron Size C Fits Well</h3>
    <p>At this height bracket, a typical popliteal height (floor to back of knee) falls between 16.5" and 18.5". The Aeron Size C's 20.5" maximum seat height covers this range comfortably — the seat can be set to allow a 90°–100° hip angle without forcing the user onto the chair's lower adjustment limit. The 8Z Pellicle mesh distributes seat pan pressure across a larger contact area than foam, which tall users in r/ergonomics consistently cite as a primary reason for choosing the Aeron. The PostureFit SL's dual-pad system aligns with the L4-S1 zone for most 6'0"–6'2" spines. One caveat: tall users report that the mesh hammock seat can subtly tilt the pelvis and flatten the lumbar curve over extended periods — if you have existing low-back sensitivity, check the <a href="/knee-pain-seat-depth/" class="link-internal">seat depth fit guide</a> to verify your thigh clearance before committing.</p>

    <h3>6'2" – 6'4": Consider Both, Lean Toward Leap Plus</h3>
    <p>This bracket is where the seat height gap between the two chairs becomes functionally significant. A 6'3" user has a typical popliteal height of approximately 18.5"–19.5". At the Aeron's 20.5" ceiling, the seat pan sits only 1"–2" above popliteal height — workable, but without margin for thicker floor mats or standing desk transitions. The Leap Plus with the optional 5" cylinder, at 22.5" max, provides 3"–4" of clearance above popliteal height, which is the adjustment buffer that ME seat-design principles call for to prevent posterior thigh compression and maintain neutral hip angle. The Leap Plus's height-adjustable lumbar also allows independent positioning at the L3-L5 zone for the longer spines typical of this bracket. Based on specs and tall-user community feedback, the Leap Plus is the lower-risk choice at 6'3"–6'4", though the Aeron remains viable if mesh breathability and brand preference are primary factors. See the <a href="/review/aeron-size-c/" class="link-internal">Aeron Size C full spec review</a> and the <a href="/review/leap-plus/" class="link-internal">Leap Plus full spec review</a> for deeper analysis of each chair individually.</p>

    <h3>6'4"+: Leap Plus Is the Clear Recommendation</h3>
    <p>Above 6'4", the Aeron Size C runs out of dimensional runway. A 6'5" user with a popliteal height of approximately 20" would need the Aeron set at or near its 20.5" maximum, leaving no adjustment margin for correct thigh-parallel positioning. At that point, any forward lean or floor mat thickness pushes the hip angle below 90° — compressing hip flexors and increasing lumbar disc load. The Leap Plus with its optional 5" cylinder, at 22.5" max, provides the 2.5" buffer needed for proper configuration; with the standard cylinder its 19.5" ceiling is below the Aeron's and offers no buffer at all. The Leap Plus's reinforced frame and 500 lb weight rating also address the structural load distribution reality for taller users: a 6'5" body at 220+ lbs applies significantly more moment arm force to the lumbar support and seat-height adjustment column than the Aeron's 350 lb-rated frame is engineered to absorb over a 12-year warranty period. For a full dimensional checklist at this height, see <a href="/correct-chair-dimensions/" class="link-internal">correct chair dimensions for tall people</a>.</p>

    <!-- LUMBAR ENGINEERING ANALYSIS -->
    <h2>Lumbar Support Engineering: PostureFit SL vs Leap Live Back</h2>

    <p>The lumbar support architecture is the second most important differentiator for tall users, after seat height. The Aeron's PostureFit SL uses a fixed dual-pad design that simultaneously contacts the sacrum and lumbar spine. The engineering rationale is sound: sacral support tilts the pelvis anteriorly, which restores natural lumbar lordosis without requiring the user to actively adjust lumbar height. For users whose spinal geometry aligns with the pad placement, this is effective. The limitation for tall users is that the pad height is fixed relative to the seat pan — and longer spines shift the L4-L5 zone higher relative to the seat, potentially misaligning the upper pad with the lumbar curve.</p>

    <p>The Steelcase Leap Plus uses what Steelcase calls the Live Back — a flexing backrest that changes shape as the user moves, combined with a separately adjustable lower back firmness dial and height-adjustable lumbar. The engineering reason this matters for tall users: independent lumbar height adjustment allows placement at the correct vertebral level regardless of torso length. Tall users in r/ergonomics who have compared both chairs frequently note that the Leap's lumbar adjustability gives them a level of fit customization that the Aeron's fixed system cannot replicate for frames above 6'2".</p>

    <!-- SEAT DEPTH NOTE -->
    <h2>Seat Depth: A Critical Fit Variable for Tall Users</h2>

    <p>Both chairs offer adjustable seat depth — the Aeron Size C from 15.75" to 17.75" and the Leap Plus from 15.5" to 18.5". For tall users, thigh length typically exceeds the general population average. A 6'3" male has a typical thigh length (greater trochanter to knee) of approximately 24"–26". Proper seat depth requires 2"–4" of clearance between the front edge of the seat pan and the back of the knee (popliteal fossa) — meaning effective seat depth for this user should target 17"–18". The Leap Plus's 18.5" maximum depth covers this range; the Aeron's 17.75" ceiling is marginally adequate. If you're experiencing knee pressure or posterior thigh numbness, the seated fit problem is almost always seat depth, not lumbar height — use the <a href="/knee-pain-seat-depth/" class="link-internal">seat depth calculator</a> to find your target number before adjusting anything else.</p>

    <!-- MESH VS FOAM NOTE -->
    <h2>Mesh vs Fabric: Comfort Tradeoffs for Long Workdays</h2>

    <p>The Aeron's 8Z Pellicle mesh eliminates the heat buildup that upholstered seats accumulate over long sessions — tall users in r/ergonomics consistently cite the Aeron's mesh as a primary selection driver for warm office environments or all-day use. The engineering basis: mesh suspends the user's mass on a tensioned membrane rather than compressing foam, distributing contact pressure over a larger surface area and allowing convective airflow across the entire seat pan. The reported downside — also confirmed by community feedback — is that older or incorrectly tensioned mesh can subtly tilt the pelvis posteriorly, reducing lumbar lordosis over time. The Leap Plus's fabric upholstery provides more consistent pressure distribution as the chair ages, though at the cost of heat retention.</p>

    <!-- PRICE AND VALUE -->
    <h2>Price, Availability, and Value Verdict</h2>

    <p>Both chairs are premium-tier purchases. New Aeron Size C chairs typically list at $1,400–$1,700 depending on configuration and retailer. The Steelcase Leap Plus is similarly priced in the $1,300–$1,800 range new. Both hold significant resale value on secondary markets, and both carry 12-year warranties — the engineering reason to care about warranty length is that seat height actuators, tilt mechanisms, and armrest adjustment systems are the highest-failure-rate components on extended-use chairs, and 12 years covers two typical office workstation replacement cycles. For tall users who prefer the secondary market, the Aeron Size C is more widely available used because the standard Aeron outsells the Leap Plus by volume — but verifying seat height cylinder condition and mesh tension is critical on any used Aeron purchase. For a broader look at what separates correctly specified chairs from undersized alternatives, see <a href="/office-chairs-for-tall-people/" class="link-internal">the complete guide to office chairs for tall people</a>.</p>

