    <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>Is the Herman Miller Aeron Size C good for people over 6'2"?</h3>
    <p>The Aeron Size C fits most users between 6'0" and 6'2" well. Its seat height tops out at 20.5" and back height reaches 25.5", which covers a 6'2" frame but begins to feel restrictive for users at 6'4"+ who need lumbar support positioned higher than the Aeron's fixed PostureFit SL allows. Tall users in r/ergonomics frequently report adequate fit at 6'2" but marginal fit above 6'3".</p>

    <h3>Does the Steelcase Leap Plus have a higher seat height than the Aeron Size C?</h3>
    <p>Yes — with one configuration caveat that decides the whole question. Ordered with the optional 5" cylinder, the Steelcase Leap Plus reaches a maximum seat height of 22.5", 2 inches higher than the Aeron Size C's 20.5" ceiling. Ordered with the standard cylinder it reaches 19.5" and is the <em>lower</em> chair of the two. For tall users with a floor-to-popliteal (back of knee) measurement above 19", a correctly specified Leap Plus provides a mechanically superior height range that prevents the forced forward-tilt and hip-flexor compression that can occur when a seat is set too low. That difference is the primary reason the Leap Plus is recommended over the Aeron Size C for users above 6'2" — and the reason the cylinder is not an upgrade to skip.</p>

    <h3>Which chair has better lumbar support for tall users — the Aeron or the Leap Plus?</h3>
    <p>For tall users, the Steelcase Leap Plus has a structural advantage in lumbar adjustability. Its lower back firmness and height-adjustable lumbar support system allows positioning at the L3-L5 vertebral zone for frames up to 6'5". The Aeron's PostureFit SL is a fixed dual-pad system that targets the sacrum and lumbar simultaneously — effective for many users but not independently height-adjustable, which can leave taller spines unsupported at the correct lordosis curve. Tall users who require lumbar placement above the standard height will find the Leap Plus's adjustability a decisive advantage.</p>

    <h3>What is the weight capacity difference between the Aeron Size C and the Leap Plus?</h3>
    <p>The Herman Miller Aeron Size C is rated to 350 lbs. The Steelcase Leap Plus is rated to 500 lbs, with a reinforced frame and wider seat designed explicitly for larger body types. For tall users who also carry more body weight — common at 6'3"+ — the Leap Plus's higher weight rating and wider 22" seat pan provide meaningful structural margin. From a mechanical engineering standpoint, taller users apply greater moment arm forces to the seat column and backrest during recline, making the Leap Plus's higher structural rating more consequential than it appears on paper.</p>

